# Regular local rings are integral domains

`IsRegularLocalRing.isDomain`: a Noetherian local ring whose maximal ideal is generated by
`ringKrullDim R` elements is an integral domain. (This is listed as a TODO in
`Mathlib/RingTheory/RegularLocalRing/Defs.lean`.)

The proof is the classical induction on the embedding dimension
`spanFinrank (maximalIdeal R)`:

* if `𝔪 ≤ 𝔪²` then `𝔪 = ⊥` and `R` is a field;
* if `𝔪` is contained in a minimal prime then that prime is `𝔪`, the prime spectrum is a
  singleton, `ringKrullDim R = 0`, and again `𝔪 = ⊥`;
* otherwise prime avoidance gives `x ∈ 𝔪 ∖ 𝔪²` outside all minimal primes, and
  `R ⧸ (x)` is again regular local with embedding dimension one less
  (`IsRegularLocalRing.quotient_span_singleton`,
  `IsRegularLocalRing.ringKrullDim_quotient_span_singleton_add_one`), so `(x)` is prime
  by induction, and a minimal prime below it must be `⊥` by Nakayama.

An identical copy of this file exists in the source at
`ReciprocityTheory/CommutativeAlgebra/MaximalIdealSquares/RingKrullDimQuotientSpanSingletonAddOne.lean`.

